index 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000..ec16676c2919270ccb2c2e129a76245e8336552b
--- /dev/null
+++ b/ssl/gm_protocol.md
@@ -0,0 +1,223 @@
+# National TLS integration
+
+This local extension is based on the current BoringSSL state machines. It does
+not load Tongsuo, use OpenSSL providers, or embed a USBKey vendor API. Applications
+select a mode on each `SSL` before beginning the handshake. Contexts and ordinary
+connections retain the upstream cipher, signature, group, and key-share defaults.
+
+## Build boundary
+
+The extension is compiled with the private `XENON_GM` preprocessor macro.
+Chromium supplies `XENON_GM=1` to BoringSSL and its internal tests only when
+`enable_gm_tls=true`. Chromium callers use the generated
+`BUILDFLAG(XENON_GM)` from the same GN argument; the library's raw define is not
+exported through its public config. Independent builds default to GM disabled
+and must define `XENON_GM=1` consistently for the library and its internal tests
+to enable the extension. Leave the macro undefined to disable it; defining it
+as zero does not disable a `defined(XENON_GM)` guard.
+
+Public constants, declarations, enum values, and object layouts remain stable
+in both configurations. With GM disabled, protocol setters reject GM modes,
+GM cipher suites and signature algorithms are not registered, SM2 groups cannot
+be configured, and the TLCP wire version is rejected. SM2 certificate and OCSP
+verification are unavailable. EVP algorithm getters and the SM2 curve getter
+return null; SM2 operations and SM3/SM4 initialization return failure. The
+void low-level SM3 transform and SM4 block functions abort if called after that
+failed initialization, rather than emitting synthetic hashes or ciphertext.
+The algorithm bodies are excluded from the disabled build, while failure stubs
+retain the public symbols.
+
+Tests exercise both configurations: enabled builds run the GM vectors and
+protocol/PKI interoperability cases; disabled builds verify rejected APIs and
+registries, rejected SM2 certificates, an ordinary ClientHello, and TLS 1.2/1.3
+handshakes with session resumption. Generated source lists remain shared between
+configurations, so their generation does not depend on a product feature flag.
+
+## Protocol boundaries
+
+* `SSL_GM_MODE_DISABLED`: the default TLS behavior. A GM session cannot be offered
+  for resumption, even if an application accidentally supplies one.
+* `SSL_GM_MODE_RFC8998`: advertise the ordinary TLS suites and the two RFC 8998
+  suites in the same ClientHello. Include an SM2 share alongside the ordinary
+  default shares to avoid an unnecessary HelloRetryRequest. Explicit share
+  configuration and server hints retain their existing semantics.
+* `SSL_GM_MODE_RFC8998_ONLY`: require TLS 1.3, an SM4 suite, curveSM2 key exchange,
+  and `sm2sig_sm3` for certificate authentication.
+* `SSL_GM_MODE_TLCP`: a separate client-only TLCP 1.1 handshake, offering static
+  ECC-SM2 with SM4-GCM-SM3 and SM4-CBC-SM3. No automatic protocol retry, TLS
+  downgrade, renegotiation, TLCP server role, or SM2DHE suite is implemented.
+
+QUIC and DTLS reject GM configuration. The existing FIPS/CNSA policies do not
+enable the new suites. The public TLCP wire/API version remains `0x0101`; the
+internal record/framing version is TLS 1.1 because TLCP uses explicit CBC IVs and
+implicit signature-algorithm fields. The cipher selects SM3 for its transcript,
+PRF, and record MAC. This internal mapping does not permit TLS 1.1 negotiation in
+TLCP mode or TLCP negotiation in ordinary TLS mode.
+
+`SSL_set1_gm_cipher_suites` can restrict an enabled mode to particular GM suites,
+for example GCM only. The restriction is enforced at advertisement, selection,
+ServerHello validation, and session resumption. It cannot enable a GM suite on
+an ordinary connection or mix TLCP suites into an RFC 8998 handshake.
+
+## ClientHello and record compatibility
+
+The static ECC TLCP ClientHello only advertises SNI, supported groups (SM2),
+ALPN, OCSP status requests, and session tickets when enabled. It does not send
+EMS, renegotiation information, EC point formats, TLS 1.3 extensions, GREASE, or
+generic TLS ClientHello padding. The signature algorithm is implicit in this
+TLCP profile, so no `signature_algorithms` extension is sent. Ordinary TLS
+ClientHello construction is unchanged.
+
+TLCP supports both session-ID and ticket resumption. In particular, the ticket
+extension is retained because replacing stateless tickets with server-side
+session-ID caches would change deployment requirements and resumption
+performance. `SSL_OP_NO_TICKET` remains an explicit caller choice. Tongsuo
+8.5.0's `ntls_extensions_clnt.c:82` and `ntls_statem_clnt.c:1614` implement the
+corresponding ticket advertisement and consumption paths.
+
+Once a validated ServerHello or HelloRetryRequest selects an RFC 8998 suite,
+the client omits the optional middlebox compatibility ChangeCipherSpec (CCS).
+This decision uses the selected cipher, not merely the enabled GM mode:
+mixed ClientHellos selecting ordinary TLS keep the upstream CCS behavior.
+The ClientHello session-ID field and the generic early-data path are unchanged;
+the application's GM connection path does not enable early data.
+[RFC 8446, Appendix D.4](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc8446#appendix-D.4)
+describes compatibility CCS. Section 5 requires receivers to ignore valid
+dummy CCS records in the permitted handshake interval; omission is a sender
+compatibility choice, not an assertion that rejecting those records is valid.
+
+Encrypted handshake messages retain BoringSSL's existing record coalescing.
+[RFC 8446, Section 5.1](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc8446#section-5.1)
+explicitly permits multiple handshake messages in one record, subject to the
+key-change boundary rules. No extra records, AEAD operations, protocol probes,
+or network round trips are added to accommodate peers that reject coalescing.
+
+`GMProtocolTLCPClientHelloExtensionsAndResumption` inspects real ClientHello
+bytes for ticket requests, ticket offers, and session-ID offers, including
+when generic TLS GREASE and SCT settings are enabled. The TLS 1.3 protocol
+tests inspect CCS and ClientHello ordering for ordinary and mixed connections,
+server-only and mutual authentication, HRR, and session resumption. Successful
+end-to-end TLCP resumption is separately covered by the Tongsuo peer tests.
+
+## Certificates and hardware keys
+
+TLCP peer certificate buffers retain the wire order: signing leaf, encryption
+leaf, issuers. A custom verifier installed with `SSL_VERIFY_PEER` must verify
+both roles, including trust, hostname, lifetime, constraints, and key usage.
+The TLS implementation separately requires SM2 leaf keys, checks signing and
+encryption key usage, and verifies that ServerKeyExchange authenticates the
+encryption certificate. The ordinary single-chain X.509 verifier is insufficient
+and is rejected for TLCP.
+
+Applications set the signing credential with `SSL_set_chain_and_key` and the
+encryption leaf with `SSL_set1_tlcp_encryption_certificate`. The latter is inserted
+after the signing leaf when the client sends its Certificate message. Async
+certificate selection and `SSL_PRIVATE_KEY_METHOD` are preserved; no PIN, SKF,
+device enumeration, or vendor-library code belongs in BoringSSL.
+
+The SM2 signing callback receives a protocol-specific message, not an ECDSA
+digest. It must apply `SM2_compute_digest` to bind the public key and identity:
+
+| Protocol operation | Callback input | SM2 identity |
+| --- | --- | --- |
+| TLS 1.3 CertificateVerify | Standard TLS 1.3 signed context and transcript hash | `TLSv1.3+GM+Cipher+Suite` |
+| TLCP client CertificateVerify | SM3 of the full handshake transcript | `1234567812345678` |
+
+TLCP server key exchange verification applies the latter identity directly to
+`client_random || server_random || uint24(enc_cert_length) || enc_cert_DER`.
+The client creates a 48-byte premaster with the TLCP version prefix and encrypts
+it with the server encryption key, using ASN.1 SM2 ciphertext in C1x/C1y/C3/C2
+order. Existing Finished, exporter, ticket, async I/O, and record mechanisms then
+use the selected SM3/SM4 algorithms.
+
+## Protocol reference and provenance
+
+The primary reference is Tongsuo 8.5.0, archive SHA-256
+`35ec0ed648e799df8b5860d3716878812f117f6563607dcdf2709023b5e337f8`:
+
+* `ssl/statem_ntls/ntls_statem_clnt.c`:
+  `tls_process_key_exchange_ntls`, `tls_construct_cke_pms_ntls`, and the
+  certificate/request parsers define the TLCP wire behavior used for comparison.
+* `ssl/statem_ntls/ntls_statem_lib.c`:
+  `x509_to_asn1_ntls` specifies the signed encryption-certificate length prefix;
+  `tls_construct_cert_verify_ntls` specifies the extra transcript digest before
+  applying the standard SM2 signature operation.
+  `ssl_add_cert_chain_ntls` (`:654`, leaf output at `:720` and `:727`) emits the
+  signing leaf followed by the encryption leaf. The client calls it through
+  `ntls_statem_clnt.c:2142`, `tls_construct_client_certificate_ntls`, with both
+  credentials. This is the reference for the local dual-client-leaf profile;
+  it is not a claim that every TLCP implementation supports that profile.
+* `ssl/statem/statem_lib.c`: `tls_construct_cert_verify` supplies the RFC 8998
+  handshake SM2 identity and preserves the TLS 1.3 signed context.
+
+The implementation is adapted to BoringSSL's `CBS`/`CBB`, `CRYPTO_BUFFER`,
+`SSL_CREDENTIAL`, transcript, and asynchronous private-key interfaces. It does
+not copy the reference provider, mutable method selection, or ASYNC machinery.
+No process-wide protocol mode is used. The old Chromium 108 patch is background
+material only; its global mode and direct SKF integration are not imported.
+
+Protocol tests in `ssl_test.cc` use the reference's Apache-2.0 test-only
+`test/certs/sm2/server_sign.crt` and `.key` as embedded fixtures. Those tests bypass
+certificate trust explicitly, while exercising actual SM2 CertificateVerify,
+ECDH, SM4 record protection, HRR, mutual authentication, and ticket resumption.
+Application PKI and independent Tongsuo interoperability tests must separately
+cover trust validation and the TLCP wire implementation.
+
+`GMProtocolTLCPReferenceRecordAndFinished` additionally checks independent
+Tongsuo 8.5.0 vectors for a complete SM4-CBC/SM3 record and both Finished values.
+It rejects changes to the wire version, sequence number, IV, and padding through
+the BoringSSL record parser. Reference CBC MAC uses sequence zero, type 23,
+version `0x0101`, MAC key `00..1f`, SM4 key `20..2f`, IV `30..3f`, and plaintext
+`TLCP record regression`. Finished vectors use secret `00..2f` and transcript
+`00..7f`, with the standard client/server labels and a 12-byte result.
+
+## Independent GmSSL interoperability limits
+
+The independent test peer is unmodified GmSSL 3.2.0, commit
+`7c9f02904ef33e59c87b4f16621cc8fd434e7579`, archive SHA-256
+`68163f2098b1c996f4aa3c4ff7d439f9782e1b65f6343eeeb9b3863d73c5e391`.
+`//net/gm/tests/run_gmssl_interop.py` verifies the archive and every reused
+upstream source file. It does not substitute a patched peer or disable
+certificate verification.
+
+The Windows x64 run on 2026-10-28 passed **2 of 4** cases: TLCP server
+authentication and RFC 8998 server authentication completed encrypted HTTP.
+Both mutual-authentication cases failed and remain failures:
+
+* **TLCP dual client leaves:** `src/tls_vrf.c:401` selects
+  `x509_certs_verify_tlcp` only for a server chain. The client-chain path calls
+  ordinary `x509_certs_verify`; `src/x509_cer.c:2021` consequently treats the
+  second client encryption leaf as a CA. `src/x509_ext.c:1411` rejects its
+  missing `keyCertSign` usage. The log identifies this leaf as serial
+  `10000005` with encryption/key-agreement usages. This is a profile mismatch
+  with the Tongsuo dual-leaf behavior described above. The test neither removes
+  that leaf nor changes its usages to make verification succeed.
+* **RFC 8998 handshake coalescing:** after successful record decryption,
+  `src/tls13.c:8672` parses the client Certificate through
+  `tls13_record_get_handshake_certificate`. `src/tls.c:1410` requires the whole
+  record payload to equal the first handshake message's length, and rejects
+  BoringSSL's coalesced Certificate/CertificateVerify/Finished flight. The
+  local `s3_both.cc`, `tls_add_message`, retains the upstream coalescing allowed
+  by RFC 8446 Section 5.1. No production framing workaround is applied.
+
+GmSSL also rejects unknown TLCP ClientHello extensions, including session
+tickets (`src/tlcp.c:1304`). These four external-fetch cases have no session
+binding and explicitly disable tickets through the test backend. Their results
+do not establish compatibility with a browser profile that enables tickets.
+The optional-CCS omission resolves an earlier GmSSL TLS 1.3 state-ordering issue,
+but does not conceal the remaining coalesced-record failure.
+
+Original evidence for this run is preserved in the local, untracked directory
+`C:/Users/Administrator/AppData/Local/CodexBuilds/chromium153-gm/gmssl-interop/results/b01623d62e9a-20261028/`:
+
+* `interop-report.json`, SHA-256
+  `2588a823b2de173a4bc465728dc7c1c5fcb5a85422833b8dc9608ba1f4524b2c`.
+* `interop.log`, SHA-256
+  `1f357de9c2e75efde1d37ecee348a9476fe1e96507dfa757387971f95a27b2e1`.
+
+The tested backend SHA-256 is
+`b01623d62e9a2630da56ad698478f6f90c72d173db78c522b1545941e13bcb27`;
+the independent peer SHA-256 is
+`a9dc5189151057ba8d52dac3c1bb29459742c5c4d589697142e10e860a71af32`.
+These results describe this exact binary and reference profile, and do not
+constitute passing independent mutual-authentication interoperability.
